Chapter: TcoShuffleSpill

 A self tail-recursive loop with five parameters. The fifth parameter
 has no local register left and lives in a spill slot, so its argument
 is not a direct tail argument and the emitter evaluates it before the
 direct moves and holds the result while they run. Three of the direct
 arguments are themselves spilled locals, and loading a spilled local
 takes a scratch register from a rotation of three, so the third such
 load hands back the register the held value is sitting in.

 When that happens the fifth parameter receives the fourth argument's
 value instead of its own: active-player is assigned next-turn, the
 alternation stops, and the turn counter never advances again. This is
 the shape that made hexwar-loop appear to hang, which is why the
 reproducer keeps the record parameter, the list parameter and the
 five-argument call -- remove any one of them and the register
 pressure changes and the fault disappears.

Section: Fixture

  Cfg = record {
   width : Integer,
   nplayers : Integer,
   limit : Integer,
   data : List Integer
  }

  Rng = record {
   state : Integer
  }

  rng-bump : Rng -> Rng
  rng-bump (r) = Rng { state = r.state + 1 }

  md : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  md (a) (b) = int-mod a b

  turn5 : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Rng -> List Integer
  turn5 (units) (data) (active) (w) (r) = units

  loop5 : Cfg, Rng, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  loop5 (cfg) (r) (units) (turn) (active) =
   if r.state >= 8 then turn
   else let r2 = rng-bump r
   in let units2 = turn5 units cfg.data active cfg.width r2
   in let next-player = md (active + 1) cfg.nplayers
   in let next-turn = if next-player == 0 then turn + 1 else turn
   in loop5 cfg r2 units2 next-turn next-player

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("turns=" & show (loop5 (Cfg { width = 4, nplayers = 2, limit = 3, data = [1, 2] }) (Rng { state = 0 }) [7, 8] 0 0))
  end
